ree videos and  articles available on YouTube and Google  also it's not like the average person is  ignorant to coding either like they were  back in the 1980s virtually everyone  understands how important computer  science is to the modern world and how  lucrative a CS career could be  considering all this you would think  that there's an oversupply of developers  yet this is not the case so what's  creating this disparity between how many  people learn coding and how many people  actually get a job in coding well at the  root of it there can only be two  explanations for this it's either the  employer's fault or the employee's fault  maybe the employers are being too picky  or expecting too much but when you look  at the data this is simply not the case  most tech companies have actually been  lowering their standards like dropping  degree requirements now you can argue  all you want that these tech companies  don't prefer college graduates but  that's besides the point this video  isn't about whether college is worth it  the point is that these tech companies  are objectively making it easier to get  a job with them some research from India  indeed it further confirms this eighty  percent of U.S tech managers have  selected candidates that graduated from  a coding boot camp program and at 99.8  percent of them are willing to hire  similar candidates again so if anything  employers are desperate and this is  extremely evident in their highly  generous compensation packages but if  the shore digit isn't caused by  employers that means that it must be  caused by employees and that's exactly  what's happening the coding industry as  a whole has a massive return problem  developers come in it they stay for a  few years and then they leave the  average tenure at all of the top  companies is literally at Max two years  now of course tenure is cured by new  hires but it's not like turnover would  be that much better anyway I mean High  turnover at these companies is not 