ortage when it comes to fast  food workers as well but due to the  limited margins in this industry  salaries can't go up all that much even  Starbucks with their six dollar coffees  only profit 10 to 15 percent not only do  tech companies have far higher margins  but they also have far fewer employees  meaning that their profit per employee  is through the roof Google's profit per  employee is 297 000 Apple's profit per  employee is 390 thousand dollars and  Facebook's profit per employee is nearly  half a million dollars the median salary  at Facebook is currently 292 thousand  dollars this means that they could  literally increase the pay of all of  their employees by four hundred thousand  dollars and it still have a hundred  thousand dollars worth of profit left to  spare in other words they could afford  to pay entry-level developers six  hundred thousand dollars if they wanted  to if we do this same math for Starbucks  though it simply doesn't work out before  Starbucks drastically reduced their  Workforce due to the pandemic their  employee counts to the 350 000 and on  good years they profit as much as six  billion dollars this however only works  out to a profit per employee of a little  over seventeen thousand dollars per year  this means that Starbucks could only  increase pay by nine dollars per hour  before having to charge like ten dollars  per cup of coffee and this is why we  don't hear about Starbucks Baristas  being page six figures due to the fast  food shortage but we do hear about  software Engineers being paid seven  figures due to the developer shortage  also that's just half the story  something else to note is that the vast  majority of the high compensations from  these tech companies are stock-based  compensation if we take a closer look at  Facebook's seven figure salary we'll see  that only about 400 000 of the 1.6  million dollars is actually offered in  cash the other 1.2 million dollars is  offered in stock not only is stock  compensation grid for employees given  that they're investing hundreds of  thousands if not Millions into the  fastest growing companies in the world  but it's also great for companies  because it's almost free here's the  thing it's not like these companies go  on to the stock market and buy these  shares at the market price instead what  they do is dilute existing shareholders  to issue new stock to employees and it  turns out that this is extraordinarily  cheap because it doesn't come from their  bottom line it comes from their market  cap for example let's say Facebook  wanted to increase their salaries by a  hundred thousand dollars to attract more  Talent if they increase the salaries of  all 72 000 employees by a hundred  thousand this would cost them a 7.2  billion dollars per year this is quite  an amount but Facebook could comfortably  afford it with their 40 billion dollar  net margin it would cost them about 18  of their profits if they offered the  hundred thousand dollars in the form of  stock though the cost would be relative  to their 393 billion dollar market cap  and 7.2 billion dollars would only cost  1.8 dilution and that's with their stock  falling off a cliff at their Peak market  cap of 1 trillion dollars this extra  compensation would have only cost a 0.66  dilution not to mention employees are  usually restricted from selling stock  compensation for a few years so this  dilution won't even hit the market for  years this is why companies that love  stock compensation that improve employee  loyalty employees make a lot of money  companies can hand out a stupid amount  and it doesn't cost them much the one  caveat to this though is that your  company has to be growing at a rate at  which this dilution would be  inconsequential so stagnant companies  like a t or Cisco or Intel can't  leverage this all that much but the fan  companies well they go to town  at the end of the day the reasoning  behind insane software engineering  salaries it isn't rocket science there's  a shortage of talented software  Engineers because talented developers  don't like to hang out at big companies  and do menial work they far prefer to  invent or build something themselves so  these companies are having to one-up  each other in terms of pay to retain  their developers and with insane profit  per employee and access to easy stock  compensation when these companies try to  outdo each other salaries basically  become a game score instead of money the  funny thing is that these companies  still have an insane amount of Headroom  honestly between sacrificing profits and  dilution I think Facebook could even  afford to pay median salaries of a  million dollars per year now I'm not  saying that they should or that they  will but they could do it automatically  so if you're looking for a  straightforward way to make a stupid  amount of money CS is definitely the way  to go but be warned that the work is not  only difficult but rather monotonous and  oftentimes unfulfilling do you think  coders are overpaid comment that down  below also drop a like if you wish you  were paid this much and of course  consider checking out our Discord  Community to suggest future video ideas  and consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then I'm Hari and I'll see you guys on  the next one  all right

